r 2025\",\"525.91\",\"36.828\",\"489.082\"],[\"03 Mar 2025\",\"751.98\",\"52.658\",\"699.322\"]]}],\"links\":[{\"h\":\"/supplier/spl_1J30PJXHY1S09VH2\",\"t\":\"Graphyte\"},{\"h\":\"https://350solutions.com\",\"t\":\"350Solutions\"},{\"h\":\"/protocol/subsurface-biomass/1.0\",\"t\":\"Subsurface Biomass Carbon Removal and Storage v1.0\"},{\"h\":\"https://storage.googleapis.com/isometric--production--eu--public-documents/spl_1J30PJXHY1S09VH2/prj_1J4P33N6W1S0RKE2/pjd_1K2MEGXZW1S0KH4G/pdd.pdf\",\"t\":\"Project Design Document | 25 Nov 2024\"},{\"h\":\"/ghg-entry/rmv_1KTQS9ZH51S0YGB2?tab=calculation-view\",\"t\":\"See GHG entry →\"},{\"h\":\"https://storage.googleapis.com/isometric--production--eu--public-documents/spl_1J30PJXHY1S09VH2/prj_1J4P33N6W1S0RKE2/pjd_1KX0NZQR71S08WSK/explanatory_note.pdf\",\"t\":\"PDF | ->\"},{\"h\":\"https://storage.googleapis.com/isometric--production--eu--public-documents/spl_1J30PJXHY1S09VH2/prj_1J4P33N6W1S0RKE2/pjd_1KX0NYMQS1S01K29/verification_report.pdf\",\"t\":\"PDF | ->\"},{\"h\":\"https://storage.googleapis.com/isometric--production--eu--public-documents/spl_1J30PJXHY1S09VH2/prj_1J4P33N6W1S0RKE2/pjd_1KX0NYB931S0T9AZ/ghg_statement_report.pdf\",\"t\":\"PDF | ->\"},{\"h\":\"https://storage.googleapis.com/isometric--production--eu--public-documents/spl_1J30PJXHY1S09VH2/prj_1J4P33N6W1S0RKE2/pjd_1KN07X5341S0YZVG/verification_report.pdf\",\"t\":\"PDF | ->\"},{\"h\":\"https://storage.googleapis.com/isometric--production--eu--public-documents/spl_1J30PJXHY1S09VH2/prj_1J4P33N6W1S0RKE2/pjd_1KN07VXTQ1S0BAZV/ghg_statement.pdf\",\"t\":\"PDF | ->\"}],\"text\":\"VALIDATED\\nBICRS\\n1,000Y+\\nLoblolly Project\\nGraphyte\\nISSUED\\n20,444.62\\nBUFFER POOL\\n1,022.246\\nCREDITING PERIOD\\n18 Aug 2024 to 17 Aug 2029\\nSummary\\nRemoval method\\nCalculation overview\\nProject documentation\\nCertificates\\nReversal risk\\nSummary\\n\\nGraphyte’s Carbon Casting technology transforms biomass residues from the agriculture and timber industries into durable carbon removal. The Carbon Casting process involves drying and compressing biomass residues into stable ‘carbon blocks.’ These blocks are wrapped in a waterproof barrier designed to prevent carbon from re-entering the atmosphere for over 1,000 years. The blocks are then stored underground in an engineered chamber with a gas detection system that directly monitors for carbon dioxide and methane. The Loblolly Project in Pine Bluff, Arkansas is the first deployment of Graphyte’s Carbon Casting technology. The Project utilizes excess sawdust, bark, and rice hulls produced from local farmers and timber companies in Southeast Arkansas.\\n\\nPROJECT DETAILS\\nCurrent verifier\\n350Solutions\\n->\\nProject code\\nV093\\nApproval date\\n07 Nov 2024\\nCurrent protocol version\\nSubsurface Biomass Carbon Removal and Storage v1.0\\nAccreditation\\n⌘ + scroll to zoom\\nLoblolly Project\\nHow carbon is being removed\\n\\nBiomass Carbon Removal and Storage (BiCRS) involves storing the carbon contained in waste biomass—organic material from living or recently living organisms. BiCRS works by halting or slowing the decomposition of biomass, enabling the carbon it contains to be stored long-term. This can be achieved by converting biomass into materials that decompose more slowly—such as biochar or bio-oil—or by storing it in environments where decomposition is slowed. For BiCRS to count as carbon removal, the biomass must be replenished to ensure additional carbon dioxide is drawn from the atmosphere.\\n\\nProject Design Document\\n25 Nov 2024\\nCalculation overview\\nCalculations demonstrating 57.58 tCO₂e removed from Loblolly Project on 04 May 2026\\nSee GHG entry →\\nNet CO₂e Removed\\n57.575 t\\nSequestrations\\n64.900 t\\n-\\nActivities\\n6.971 tCO₂e\\n-\\nUncertainty Discount\\n353 kg\\n⌘ + scroll to zoom\\nView full screen\\nProject documentation\\nSubmission date\\tTitle\\tDownload\\n08 Jul 2026\\tExplanatory Note\\tPDF\\n->\\n\\n08 Jul 2026\\tVerification Report\\tPDF\\n->\\n\\n08 Jul 2026\\tGHG Statement Report\\tPDF\\n->\\n\\n30 Mar 2026\\tVerification Report\\tPDF\\n->\\n\\n30 Mar 2026\\tGHG Statement\\tPDF\\n->\\nShowing 5 of 22\\nPrevious\\nNext\\nCertificates\\nIssued\\n20,444.62\\nRetired\\n13,237.05\\nBuffer pool\\n1,022.246\\nCrediting period\\n18 Aug 2024 to 17 Aug 2029\\nIssuances\\nRetirements\\nRefunds\\nIssue date\\tIssued\\tBuffer pool\\tSupplier allocation\\n08 Jul 2026\\t3,839.12\\t191.971\\t3,647.149\\n30 Mar 2026\\t1,454.13\\t101.802\\t1,352.328\\n27 Feb 2026\\t2,041.55\\t142.926\\t1,898.624\\n27 Feb 2026\\t1,880.24\\t131.634\\t1,748.606\\n22 Dec 2025\\t2,302.76\\t161.215\\t2,141.545\\n19 Dec 2025\\t2,285.27\\t160\\t2,125.27\\n01 Oct 2025\\t2,546.09\\t178.265\\t2,367.825\\n27 Jun 2025\\t1,868.15\\t130.801\\t1,737.349\\n03 Mar 2025\\t525.91\\t36.828\\t489.082\\n03 Mar 2025\\t751.98\\t52.658\\t699.322\\nShowing 10 of 12\\nPrevious\\nNext\\nReversal risk\\n\\nThe risk of reversal for this project has been assessed as low. This means that 5% of certificates issued for this project are allocated to Graphyte's buffer pool.\\n\\nTo date, this project has contributed 1,022.246 certificates to the buffer pool which holds a total of 1,022.246 certificates.\\n\\nGraphyte has no reversals to date.\\n\\nLearn more about reversal risk\"}"